{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-65181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-65181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}=\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-65181" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d94ea6cee5998988a97c6824fe3d61728fb62031b3e6c680f844842ec7bd46bd"} {"question" "q-65181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-65181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "2{sin}^{2}⁡x"] ["math" "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-65181" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-344110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-344110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/assets/1b564f91cb71a17a1449e1ff723e32f450da6304d9c3ba33cd01e2ff942bc25c.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\infty .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-344110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d94ea6cee5998988a97c6824fe3d61728fb62031b3e6c680f844842ec7bd46bd"} {"question" "q-344110" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-344110" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-109152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ for the curve $5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-109152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3}-2{y}^{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{4}) & =0 \\\\ 15{x}^{2}-8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -8{y}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-15{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{8{y}^{3}})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot {1}^{2}}{8\\cdot {1}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-109152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d94ea6cee5998988a97c6824fe3d61728fb62031b3e6c680f844842ec7bd46bd"} {"question" "q-109152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-109152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-109152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{15}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-332518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-332518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-332518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d94ea6cee5998988a97c6824fe3d61728fb62031b3e6c680f844842ec7bd46bd"} {"question" "q-332518" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-332518" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-332518" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-332518" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-65951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-65951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-1$ and adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\ge 1+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-1$ and adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\le -1+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get \n\n$$\nf(x)\\ge 2,\\,f(x)\\le 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-65951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d94ea6cee5998988a97c6824fe3d61728fb62031b3e6c680f844842ec7bd46bd"} {"question" "q-65951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-65951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [2,\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [0,\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [1,\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-65951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-12019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\int \\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-12019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3} & =3\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)}{u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-3x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-3x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-3x)+0 \\\\  & =-(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3(1-3x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-12019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d94ea6cee5998988a97c6824fe3d61728fb62031b3e6c680f844842ec7bd46bd"} {"question" "q-12019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-12019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}+C"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3(1-3x)^{2}}+C"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-12019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-332057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A data set has a mean $\\overset{―}{x}=6.2$ and an unknown standard deviation. An observation of the data set has the value $x=5.08$ and $z$-score $z=-0.7.$ Find the standard deviation of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-332057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5.08,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=6.2,$ and $z=-0.7$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.08 & =6.2+(-0.7)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -1.12 & =-0.7\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/state.json" "file_sha256" "33a6876d2de1795884e9b8e9084a0b2cf2780a7124231c4ba31cbc5b764d003b"} {"question" "q-332057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009686/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d94ea6cee5998988a97c6824fe3d61728fb62031b3e6c680f844842ec7bd46bd"} {"question" "q-332057" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/t